## Per-Proposal File Output

For each proposal, write its **full detailed content** to an individual file at:
`{{ workspace_outputs }}/proposals/<ID>.md` (e.g., `proposals/P1.md`).

Each proposal file should be a complete, self-contained document covering
the proposal in depth — including but not limited to problem context, approach
details, implementation considerations, impact analysis, risks, and any
supporting evidence or references gathered during research.

The unified plan artifact still contains the integrated narrative. The per-proposal
files provide the full detail that downstream tools need when picking individual
proposals for execution.
{%- endif %}

## Structured Proposal Index

Conclude your deliverable (`{{ output_path }}`) with a structured proposal index as a fenced JSON block — it rides IN the deliverable file (a downstream tool parses the index directly from the file, not from your `<Response>` summary).
Each proposal must be individually addressable by a unique ID (use sequential
IDs like P1, P2, ...). Group proposals into phases by implementation priority.
{%- if separate_proposal_files %}

The `proposal_file` field MUST point to the per-proposal file you wrote above.
{%- endif %}

```json proposal_index
{
  "version": "1",
  "themes": ["Multi-Task Architecture", "Loss & Training"],
  "groups": [
    {
      "phase": 1,
      "label": "Quick Wins",
      "description": "Low complexity, high impact changes",
      "proposals": [
        {
          "id": "P1",
          "rank": 1,
          "title": "Short descriptive title",
          "summary": "One-line summary of the proposal",
          "impact": "high",
          "complexity": "low",
          "problem": "What problem this solves",
          "approach": "How to implement it",
{%- if separate_proposal_files %}
          "proposal_file": "proposals/P1.md",
{%- endif %}
          "dependencies": [],
          "cross_refs": "Builds on the shared registry from P3",
          "tags": ["category"]
        }
      ],
      "batches": [
        {
          "id": "1A",
          "label": "Loss & Training, Independent",
          "timeline": "Week 1",
          "proposal_ids": ["P1"]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "conflicts",
      "proposal_ids": ["P2", "P4"],
      "reason": "Both modify losses/sampled_softmax.py; apply sequentially, not in parallel"
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "mutually_exclusive",
      "proposal_ids": ["P5", "P6"],
      "reason": "Two alternative approaches to the same goal; pick one"
    }
  ],
  "total_count": 1
}
```

**Rules for the proposal index:**
- Every proposal from the unified plan MUST appear in exactly one group.
- `rank` is globally unique across all groups (1 = highest priority).
- `summary` MUST be 1-2 sentences of plain prose. NO markdown headings, tables, bullet lists, code fences, or bold-only fragments. Do NOT truncate — never end with `…` or `...`. Full detail belongs in the per-proposal document ({%- if separate_proposal_files %} `proposal_file`{%- else %} unified plan body{%- endif %}), not this field. The widget uses `summary` verbatim as the collapsed-card one-liner.
- `problem`, `approach`, and any prose field: write complete sentences. Do NOT truncate with `…` — the JSON prose fields are shown inline in the widget, and truncation markers surface as visible garbage.
{%- if separate_proposal_files %}
- `proposal_file` points to the per-proposal detail file (relative to workspace outputs).
{%- endif %}
- `dependencies` lists proposal IDs that must be implemented before the current proposal; these may reference proposals in any group.
- `cross_refs` captures informal, free-text context notes that don't fit the structured fields (e.g. "builds on the registry introduced in P3"). Do NOT restate conflicts or alternatives here — those belong in `constraints`.
- `constraints` captures formal inter-proposal rules. Each entry has an `id`, a `kind`, the `proposal_ids` it applies to, and a short `reason`. Valid `kind` values:
  - `conflicts` — the proposals **clash if applied together** (e.g. they modify the same file/function), so implement them **sequentially, not in parallel**. BOTH can still be selected.
  - `mutually_exclusive` — the proposals are **alternatives**; pick only **one**.
- Actively derive `conflicts`: compare proposals' impacted files/modules — proposals touching the same file/function should get a `conflicts` constraint unless they are cleanly separable.
- `total_count` must equal the actual number of proposals.
- `themes` (optional) lists the cross-cutting theme labels proposals fall under; the Experiment Hub uses these to group proposals by theme. Omit if not applicable.
- `batches` (optional, per group) sub-divides a phase into executable batches; each batch carries an `id`, optional `label`/`timeline`, and `proposal_ids` referencing proposals in that group. The Experiment Hub queues selected proposals batch-by-batch. Proposals that pair well and should be done together go into the same batch. Omit if you are not batching.
